## Proof

We evaluate the contour integral
$$I = \frac{1}{2\pi i}\oint_{|z|=1}\frac{e^{\frac{c(z-z^{-1})}{2}}}{z^{n+1}}\,dz$$
by substituting the unit-circle parametrization $z = e^{i\theta}$, $\theta\in[0,2\pi)$, with $dz = ie^{i\theta}\,d\theta$.

### Step 1: Parametrize the contour

With $z=e^{i\theta}$ we have $z^{-1}=e^{-i\theta}$ and
$$\frac{z-z^{-1}}{2} = \frac{e^{i\theta}-e^{-i\theta}}{2} = i\sin\theta,$$
so the exponential factor becomes
$$e^{\frac{c(z-z^{-1})}{2}} = e^{ic\sin\theta}.$$
Also $z^{n+1}=e^{i(n+1)\theta}$ and $dz = ie^{i\theta}\,d\theta$. Substituting:
$$I = \frac{1}{2\pi i}\int_0^{2\pi}\frac{e^{ic\sin\theta}}{e^{i(n+1)\theta}}\,ie^{i\theta}\,d\theta = \frac{1}{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}e^{i(c\sin\theta - n\theta)}\,d\theta.$$

### Step 2: Separate real and imaginary parts

Write
$$I = \frac{1}{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}\cos(c\sin\theta-n\theta)\,d\theta + \frac{i}{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}\sin(c\sin\theta-n\theta)\,d\theta.$$

**The imaginary part vanishes by odd symmetry.** Using $2\pi$-periodicity, shift the integration domain to $[-\pi,\pi]$. Under the substitution $\theta\mapsto -\theta$:
- $\sin\theta\mapsto -\sin\theta$, so $c\sin\theta\mapsto -c\sin\theta$;
- $n\theta\mapsto -n\theta$;
- hence $c\sin\theta-n\theta\mapsto -(c\sin\theta-n\theta)$;
- so $\sin(c\sin\theta-n\theta)\mapsto -\sin(c\sin\theta-n\theta)$.

The integrand of the imaginary part is odd, so
$$\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\sin(c\sin\theta-n\theta)\,d\theta = 0.$$

Therefore
$$I = \frac{1}{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}\cos(c\sin\theta-n\theta)\,d\theta,$$
which is manifestly real.

### Step 3: Identify the result via the Jacobi–Anger expansion

The Jacobi–Anger expansion gives
$$e^{ic\sin\theta} = \sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty}J_k(c)\,e^{ik\theta},$$
where $J_k$ is the Bessel function of the first kind of order $k$. Substituting into the integral:
$$I = \frac{1}{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}\left(\sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty}J_k(c)\,e^{ik\theta}\right)e^{-in\theta}\,d\theta = \sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty}J_k(c)\underbrace{\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}e^{i(k-n)\theta}\,d\theta}_{\displaystyle =\,\delta_{k,n}}.$$

The orthogonality of the complex exponentials over $[0,2\pi]$ (a consequence of $2\pi$-periodicity) collapses the sum to the single term $k=n$:
$$I = J_n(c).$$

### Conclusion

$$\boxed{\frac{1}{2\pi i}\oint_{|z|=1}\frac{e^{\frac{c(z-z^{-1})}{2}}}{z^{n+1}}\,dz = J_n(c),}$$
the Bessel function of the first kind of order $n$. The $2\pi$-periodicity of the integrand allows the domain shift to $[-\pi,\pi]$, where the odd symmetry $\theta\mapsto -\theta$ kills the imaginary (sine) part, leaving the real cosine integral that is exactly the standard integral representation of $J_n(c)$.

